WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.708
The busy house has two ways to tell people things.

2
00:00:05.708 --> 00:00:09.981
In the hall hangs the menu board, which always shows the latest choice.

3
00:00:09.981 --> 00:00:17.803
In the kitchen hangs the house bell, which the cook rings for each announcement: soup's ready, the bread is here, clear the table.

4
00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:20.797
Tonight the bell has a small slate beside it.

5
00:00:20.797 --> 00:00:26.077
The cook chalks each announcement on it, so that a listener who was busy can catch up.

6
00:00:26.077 --> 00:00:35.227
Two people listen: the waiter, who is quick, and the diary keeper, who copies every announcement into the kitchen diary by hand, one second per line.

7
00:00:35.433 --> 00:00:38.490
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

8
00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:43.169
If only the diary keeper is slow, surely the quick waiter hears everything?

9
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:53.721
In code, the bell is a MutableSharedFlow, a hot flow: it runs whether or not anyone listens, and shares each value with whoever is listening at that moment.

10
00:00:53.721 --> 00:00:55.628
Each listener is a subscriber.

11
00:00:55.628 --> 00:01:03.132
The helper gave the slate room for two announcements, and chose DROP_LATEST: when the slate is full, drop the new one.

12
00:01:03.132 --> 00:01:07.012
Then the cook rings six announcements, 10 milliseconds apart.

13
00:01:07.012 --> 00:01:08.601
What does the waiter get?

14
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:12.599
1, 2 and 3.

15
00:01:12.599 --> 00:01:16.413
He lost 4, 5 and 6, and he was never slow.

16
00:01:16.633 --> 00:01:17.721
Look at the slate.

17
00:01:17.721 --> 00:01:23.827
There is one slate, and every listener reads it at their own pace, keeping their place with a finger.

18
00:01:23.827 --> 00:01:27.273
A line can be wiped only when every listener has read it.

19
00:01:27.273 --> 00:01:31.444
So the slowest listener decides when the slate is full, for everyone.

20
00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:33.087
Let's walk through it.

21
00:01:33.087 --> 00:01:37.219
The diary keeper takes 1, and starts a line that takes a second.

22
00:01:37.219 --> 00:01:38.768
2 and 3 go on the slate.

23
00:01:38.768 --> 00:01:44.514
The waiter reads them at once, but that wipes nothing: the diary keeper still needs them.

24
00:01:44.514 --> 00:01:48.646
Then 4 finds the slate full, and DROP_LATEST never chalks it up.

25
00:01:48.646 --> 00:01:51.035
Nobody hears it, the waiter included.

26
00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:55.452
The other two policies make other choices.

27
00:01:55.452 --> 00:02:03.009
SUSPEND, the default, makes the cook wait with the chalk raised until the diary keeper takes 2, at one second.

28
00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:06.376
Both get all six, but the quick waiter waits too.

29
00:02:06.376 --> 00:02:12.903
DROP_OLDEST chalks 4 over 2, so only the slow listener skips: the diary keeper gets 1, 5 and 6.

30
00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:16.939
One slow pen, and the whole house either waits or goes deaf.

31
00:02:16.939 --> 00:02:20.300
So the overflow policy is never about one subscriber.

32
00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.711
It's a choice for all of them at once.

33
00:02:22.933 --> 00:02:31.108
If nothing may be lost and the waiter must not wait, give the diary keeper a shelf of his own: buffer of 64 on his side.

34
00:02:31.108 --> 00:02:33.970
He copies each line off the slate at once.

35
00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:38.057
Both get all six, and every emit returns by 50 milliseconds.

36
00:02:38.267 --> 00:02:39.518
So here's the rule.

37
00:02:39.518 --> 00:02:42.943
A SharedFlow has one buffer for all its subscribers.

38
00:02:42.943 --> 00:02:45.775
Give a slow subscriber a buffer of its own.

39
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:52.693
The menu board is the other kind of hot flow, a StateFlow: one slot that holds the current value.

40
00:02:52.693 --> 00:02:58.283
Each reader remembers the last card they read, and reacts only if the board is different.

41
00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:59.775
The soup is tomato.

42
00:02:59.775 --> 00:03:02.997
A reader needs 100 milliseconds for each change.

43
00:03:02.997 --> 00:03:06.689
Meanwhile the soup becomes leek, and then tomato again.

44
00:03:06.689 --> 00:03:08.703
What does the busy reader see?

45
00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:12.849
Just tomato, once.

46
00:03:12.849 --> 00:03:17.572
The reader looked up, saw tomato, and concluded that nothing had happened.

47
00:03:17.572 --> 00:03:20.061
As far as the board knows, nothing had.

48
00:03:20.267 --> 00:03:22.967
So a StateFlow keeps state, not history.

49
00:03:22.967 --> 00:03:25.869
When every change counts, use a SharedFlow.

50
00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:30.549
Most hot flows in real programs are a cold flow, shared.

51
00:03:30.549 --> 00:03:40.857
stateIn and shareIn read like conversions, but each one launches a coroutine in your scope: one cook who cooks the recipe once and serves every diner.

52
00:03:41.067 --> 00:03:43.779
The started setting decides when she works.

53
00:03:43.779 --> 00:03:45.987
Eagerly starts her at opening time.

54
00:03:45.987 --> 00:03:49.709
Lazily lets the first diner start her, and she never stops.

55
00:03:49.709 --> 00:03:59.739
WhileSubscribed of 5,000 keeps her cooking while anyone is at the table, and stops her 5 seconds after the last diner leaves, in case someone just stepped out.

56
00:04:00.467 --> 00:04:05.415
Here's the lesson's run, with the oven thermometer and one screen, A.

57
00:04:05.415 --> 00:04:08.786
A switches off at 2.5 seconds and is back at 4.

58
00:04:08.786 --> 00:04:12.945
That's inside 5 seconds, so it's the same run: A gets 104.

59
00:04:13.167 --> 00:04:16.720
A goes off again at 6, and the stop comes at 11.

60
00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:25.084
When A comes back at 14, it first gets the stale 111, and then 200, from a second run, cooked again from the top.

61
00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:28.283
One trap.

62
00:04:28.283 --> 00:04:32.816
Reading the value is just glancing at the hall board from the doorway.

63
00:04:32.816 --> 00:04:34.434
It doesn't call the cook.

64
00:04:34.434 --> 00:04:42.011
A board built with WhileSubscribed and only read through its value never starts: three glances read null, null, null.

65
00:04:42.011 --> 00:04:47.061
So the lab's OvenBoard, which people only read, starts its hall board Eagerly.

66
00:04:47.867 --> 00:04:50.541
So, does a slow listener only hurt himself?

67
00:04:50.541 --> 00:04:51.785
Not on a SharedFlow.

68
00:04:51.785 --> 00:04:57.073
There's one slate, and the slowest reader sets the pace, or the losses, for everyone.

69
00:04:57.073 --> 00:05:01.178
The menu board keeps one slot and skips what a busy reader missed.

70
00:05:01.178 --> 00:05:05.905
And a shared flow is one cook, started and stopped by who sits at the table.

71
00:05:06.133 --> 00:05:07.015
Quick check.

72
00:05:07.015 --> 00:05:13.260
Under WhileSubscribed of 5,000, a screen goes off at 10 seconds and comes back at 13.

73
00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:15.391
Does the thermometer restart?

74
00:05:18.367 --> 00:05:18.588
No.

75
00:05:18.588 --> 00:05:24.716
Coming back within 5 seconds cancels the pending stop, so the same run keeps going.
